TEST805_ROW {"case_id": "Q1", "question": "According to the article Ten simple rules for getting and giving credit for data, what role do persistent identifiers play in crediting scientific data?", "controller": {"action": "ANSWER", "answer": "According to the article \"Ten simple rules for getting and giving credit for data,\" persistent identifiers (PIDs) play a crucial role in crediting scientific data by ensuring that data are findable. Specifically, PIDs, such as digital object identifiers (DOIs), provide a unique, universal, and machine-readable way to reference data, which allows the data to be consistently and reliably identified, even if the URL for the resource changes. This makes it easier for others to cite and give credit to the data contributors. Additionally, PIDs should be registered and indexable, which helps them be discovered by search engines, further enhancing the discoverability and creditability of the data.", "selected": ["PMC9521804", "PMC9401135"], "route_method": "TITLE_LEXICAL_BASELINE_NOT_AUTONOMOUS", "scores": [{"id": "PMC9521804", "score": 2.64575}, {"id": "PMC9401135", "score": 0.37796}], "steps": [{"operation": "PAIR", "ids": ["PMC9521804", "PMC9401135"], "raw": "According to the article \"Ten simple rules for getting and giving credit for data,\" persistent identifiers (PIDs) play a crucial role in crediting scientific data by ensuring that data are findable. Specifically, PIDs, such as digital object identifiers (DOIs), provide a unique, universal, and machine-readable way to reference data, which allows the data to be consistently and reliably identified, even if the URL for the resource changes. This makes it easier for others to cite and give credit to the data contributors. Additionally, PIDs should be registered and indexable, which helps them be discovered by search engines, further enhancing the discoverability and creditability of the data.", "tokens": 136, "max_new": 160, "possible_truncation": false, "sentence_end": true, "sdpa_calls": 544, "elapsed_seconds": 30.91}, {"operation": "SINGLE", "ids": ["PMC9521804"], "raw": "According to the article, persistent identifiers (PIDs) play a crucial role in crediting scientific data by ensuring that data are findable, a key principle of the FAIR data guidelines. PIDs, such as digital object identifiers (DOIs), uniquely identify data, making them globally and universally recognizable. They are machine-readable, which allows for automated attribution, and they remain constant even if the URL for the resource changes. This stability and uniqueness help in making data more discoverable and easier to reference, thereby facilitating proper credit for data contributors. Additionally, PIDs should be registered and indexable, so they can be discovered by search engines, further enhancing the visibility and creditability of the data.", "tokens": 141, "max_new": 160, "possible_truncation": false, "sentence_end": true, "sdpa_calls": 564, "elapsed_seconds": 24.252}, {"operation": "REVERSE_PAIR", "ids": ["PMC9401135", "PMC9521804"], "raw": "According to the article \"Ten simple rules for getting and giving credit for data,\" persistent identifiers (PIDs) play a crucial role in crediting scientific data by ensuring that data are findable. PIDs, such as digital object identifiers (DOIs), are universal, globally unique, and machine-readable, which allows resources to be referenced by a single identifier. This means that even if the URL for the resource changes, the identifier remains constant, enabling the data to be reliably discovered and cited. PIDs should also be registered and indexable, so they can be found by search engines, further enhancing the discoverability and creditability of the data.", "tokens": 131, "max_new": 160, "possible_truncation": false, "sentence_end": true, "sdpa_calls": 524, "elapsed_seconds": 29.918}], "retry": false, "complete_under_budget": true, "pair_sentence_end": true, "all_sentence_end": true, "lexical_agreement": {"pair_single": 0.4567901234567901, "pair_reverse": 0.5857142857142857, "pair_alternate": null}, "warning": "Lexical agreement and sentence ending do not prove scientific accuracy."}}
